ections is still one edge from each man and one edge from the girl's perspective. Therefore, the total count remains balanced.\n\nAlternatively, suppose we think in terms of linear algebra. If we create an adjacency matrix where rows represent short men and columns represent little girls, with a 1 indicating a \"knows\" relationship. Each row (short man) has exactly 6 ones, and each column (little girl) has exactly 6 ones. The total number of ones in the matrix is 6|M| (from rows) and also 6|G| (from columns). Since the total number of ones must be the same regardless of whether we count by rows or columns, it follows that 6|M| = 6|G|, so |M| = |G|.\n\nHmm, all these different approaches\u2014graph theory, linear algebra\u2014lead to the same conclusion. I can't think of any scenario where the numbers wouldn't balance out, given the regularity of the graph on both partitions. Unless there's some hidden constraint or the problem isn't accounting for something, like people knowing others outside the city or something, but the problem states \"in Flower City,\" so we can assume all relationships are within the city.\n\nAnother check: if there were, say, 2 short men and each knows 6 girls, but there are only 3 girls, each girl would have to know 4 short men (since total edges would be 12, divided by 3 girls gives 4 each). But in the problem, each girl must know exactly 6, so that scenario isn't possible. Thus, the numbers must align so that 6|M| = 6|G|, hence |M| = |G|.\n\nTherefore, after considering various perspectives and verifying through different mathematical frameworks, the conclusion holds. The number of short men must equal the number of little girls in Flower City.", "deepseek_solution": "The number of short men in Flower City is equal to the number of little girls. Here's the proof:\n\n1. **Model the problem as a bipartite graph**:\n   - Let \\( M \\) be the set of short men and \\( G \\) the set of little girls.\n   - Edges represent mutual \"knows\" relati