 3B + 3C = 540\u00b0, which is 3*180\u00b0, so each of the angles 3A, 3B, 3C is three times an angle in a triangle. However, since one of the original angles is 60\u00b0, one of the tripled angles will be 180\u00b0, right? Wait, if angle A is 60\u00b0, then 3A = 180\u00b0, which makes sin 3A = sin 180\u00b0 = 0. That might simplify things. So maybe if one angle is 60\u00b0, then the corresponding tripled angle sine term becomes zero, and we only have to compute the sum of the sines of the other two tripled angles.\n\nBut let me check this. Suppose angle A is 60\u00b0, so 3A = 180\u00b0, sin 3A = 0. Then we need to compute sin 3B + sin 3C. Since B + C = 120\u00b0, then 3B + 3C = 360\u00b0, so 3C = 360\u00b0 - 3B. Therefore, sin 3C = sin (360\u00b0 - 3B) = -sin 3B. Therefore, sin 3B + sin 3C = sin 3B - sin 3B = 0. Wait, so does that mean the entire sum is 0 + 0 = 0? But that seems too straightforward. Let me verify.\n\nIf A = 60\u00b0, then B + C = 120\u00b0, so 3B + 3C = 360\u00b0, hence 3C = 360\u00b0 - 3B. Then sin 3C = sin(360\u00b0 - 3B) = -sin 3B, since sine is negative in the fourth quadrant. Therefore, sin 3B + sin 3C = sin 3B - sin 3B = 0. Therefore, sin 3A + sin 3B + sin 3C = 0 + 0 = 0.\n\nWait, but that would mean regardless of the values of B and C, as long as their sum is 120\u00b0, their tripled angles' sines cancel each other out. That seems correct. Let me test with a specific example. Suppose A = 60\u00b0, B = 60\u00b0, then C = 60\u00b0, making it an equilateral triangle. Then sin 3A = sin 180\u00b0 = 0, sin 3B = sin 180\u00b0 = 0, sin 3C = sin 180\u00b0 = 0. So the sum is 0, which matches.\n\nAnother example: Let A = 60\u00b0, B = 90\u00b0, then C = 30\u00b0. Then 3A = 180\u00b0, sin 3A = 0. 3B = 270\u00b0, sin 270\u00b0 = -1. 3C = 90\u00b0, sin 90\u00b0 = 1. Then sum is 0 + (-1) + 1 = 0. Okay, that works too.\n\nAnother case: A = 60\u00b0, B = 30\u00b0, C = 90\u00b0. Then same as above, sin 3B = sin 90\u00b0 = 1, sin 3C = sin 270\u00b0 = -1. Sum is