ir.\n\nWait, but how was this achieved? Let me see. The key is that the girls' starting positions are arranged such that their cyclic movement covers the boys in such a way that in each subsequent dance, at least one girl's current position coincides with her partner's seat.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a permutation problem. The initial assignment is a permutation of the boys, where each girl is assigned a boy (possibly her partner). Then, each subsequent dance is a cyclic shift of this permutation. So if we represent the initial permutation as a function \u03c3, then the permutations for the dances are \u03c3, \u03c3\u2218c, \u03c3\u2218c\u00b2, ..., where c is the cyclic permutation that shifts each boy counterclockwise.\n\nWe need that for each k \u2265 0, the permutation \u03c3\u2218c^k has at least one fixed point (i.e., at least one girl is assigned her partner in the k-th dance).\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding a permutation \u03c3 of the boys such that for each k from 0 to n-1 (since after n shifts, it cycles back), \u03c3\u2218c^k has at least one fixed point. Then, since the problem requires at least n dances, but the pattern repeats every n dances, so we need this condition to hold for all k.\n\nSo, in permutation terms, we need a permutation \u03c3 such that \u03c3\u2218c^k has at least one fixed point for all k = 0, 1, ..., n-1.\n\nNow, the question becomes: for which n does there exist a permutation \u03c3 of n elements such that for all k, \u03c3 shifted cyclically by k positions (counterclockwise) has at least one fixed point?\n\nAlternatively, considering that shifting the permutation \u03c3 by k positions is equivalent to composing \u03c3 with the cyclic permutation c^k. So \u03c3\u2218c^k is the permutation where each element is first shifted by k positions counterclockwise, then permuted by \u03c3.\n\nBut perhaps another way to look at it is that if we think of the boys arranged in a circle, and each girl is assign