\) or \\((-4, \\pm 6)\\), the triangle is right-angled with hypotenuse 10. The circumradius \\( R \\) is half the hypotenuse:\n     \\[\n     R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5\n     \\]\n\nThus, the circumradius \\( R \\) of \\( \\triangle F_1PF_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n1. Given that \\( F_1 \\) and \\( F_2 \\) are the foci of the hyperbola \\( C: \\frac{x^2}{4} - \\frac{y^2}{12} = 1 \\). \n\n2. The equation of the hyperbola is in standard form \\( \\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1 \\) with \\( a^2 = 4 \\) and \\( b^2 = 12 \\).\n\n3. The distance between the foci \\( |F_1 F_2| \\) is given by \\( 2c \\) where \\( c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 12} = \\sqrt{16} = 4 \\).\n\n4. Hence, the distance between the foci, \\( |F_1 F_2| = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8 \\).\n\n5. Let \\( P(x_0, y_0) \\) be a point on the hyperbola. \n\n6. The distances \\( |PF_1| = r_1 \\) and \\( |PF_2| = r_2 \\) satisfy the hyperbola's property \\( |r_1 - r_2| = 4 \\).\n\n7. Given that the centroid \\( G \\) and incenter \\( I \\) of \\( \\triangle F_1 P F_2 \\) have the property \\( G I \\parallel x \\)-axis.\n\n8. The radius \\( r \\) of the incenter \\( \\triangle F_1 P F_2 \\) is given by \\( r = \\frac{1}{3} y_0 \\).\n\n9. Therefore, the area \\( S_{\\triangle F_1 P F_2} \\) using the inradius formula is:\n    \\[\n    S_{\\triangle F_1 P F_2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( |F_1 P| + |F_1 F_2| + |F_2 P| \\right) \\cdot r = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( r_1 + r_2 + 8 \\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} y_0\n    \\]\n\n10. Also, the area \\( S_{\\triangle F_1 P F_2} \\) can be calculated directly as:\n    \\[\n    S_{\\triangle F_1 P F_2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot |F_1 F_2| \\cdot y_0 = 4y_0\n    \\]\n\n11. Equating the two expressions for the area:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{2} \\left( r_1 + r_2 + 8 \\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} y_0 = 4y_0\n    \\]\n\n12. Simplifying, we obtain:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{1}{6} \\left( r_1 + r_2 + 8 \\right) = 4\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    r_1 + r_2 + 8 = 24\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    r_1 + r_2 = 16\n    \\]\n\n13. Combining this with \\( r_1 - r_2 = 4 \\), solving for \\( r_1 \\) and \\( r_2 \\):\n    \\[\n    r_1 + r_2 = 16\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    r_1 - r_2 = 4\n    \\]\n    Adding these equations:\n    \\[\n    2r_1 = 20 \\implies r_1 = 10\n    \\]\n    Subtracting the second equation from the first:\n    \\[\n    2r_2 = 12 \\implies r_2 = 6\n    \\]\n\n14. We have \\( |F_1 P| = 10 \\) and \\( |F_2 P| = 6 \\).\n\n15. By the Pythagorean theorem, \\( |F_1 P|^2 = |F_1 F_2|^2 + |F_2 P|^2 \\), and since \n    \\( |F_1 P| = 10 \\), \\( |F_2 P| = 6 \\), and \\( |F_1 F_2| = 8 \\):\n\n    \\[\n    10^2 = 8^2 + 6^2\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    100 = 64 + 36\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    100 = 100\n    \\]\n\n16. Therefore, \\( \\triangle F_1 P F_2 \\) is a right triangle with \\( P F_2 \\perp F_1 F_2 \\).\n\n17. Thus, the circumradius \\( R \\) of the right triangle \\( \\triangle F_1 P F_2 \\) is:\n    \\[\n    R = \\frac{1}{2} |F_1 P| = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 = 5\n    \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "How many different right-angled triangles exist, one of the legs of which is \\(\\sqrt{2016}\\), and the other leg and hypotenuse are expressed in natural numbers?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many different right-angled triangles exist where one of the legs is \u221a2016, and the other leg and hypotenuse are natural numbers. Hmm, right-angled triangles... Pythagoras theorem, right? So, if the legs are a and b, and hypotenuse is c, then a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = c\u00b2. Here, one leg is \u221a2016, so let me denote that. Let's say a = \u221a2016, then the other leg b and hypotenuse c must be natural numbers. So the equation becomes (\u221a2016)\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = c\u00b2. Simplifying that, 2016 + b\u00b2 = c\u00b2. Then, rearranging, c\u00b2 - b\u00b2 = 2016. \n\nWait, c\u00b2 - b\u00b2 factors into (c - b)(c + b) = 2016. So, I need to f