eed to find the factor pairs $(n, k)$ of 2016, where $n = c-b$ and $k = c+b$. \n\n4. Since \\(2016\\) can be written as its prime factorization:\n   \\[\n   2016 = 2^5 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n\n5. We need each factor pair $(n, k)$ such that $n$ and $k$ are both even to ensure $c$ and $b$ can both be integers.\n\n6. The system of equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   c - b = n \\\\\n   c + b = k\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   can be solved by:\n\n   \\[\n   c = \\frac{n + k}{2}, \\quad b = \\frac{k - n}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For $c$ and $b$ to be integers, $n$ and $k$ must both be even.\n\n7. We now consider all possible even factor pairs of 2016:\n   \\[\n   2, \\, 4, \\, 6, \\, 8, \\, 12, \\, 14, \\, 16, \\, 18, \\, 24, \\, 28, \\, 36, \\, 44\n   \\]\n\n8. Check if the factor pairs listed ensure $c$ and $b$ are integers:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n   \\hline\n   n & k = \\frac{2016}{n} & (n, k) \\\\\n   \\hline\n   2 & 1008 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   4 & 504 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   6 & 336 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   8 & 252 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   12 & 168 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   14 & 144 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   16 & 126 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   18 & 112 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   24 & 84 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   28 & 72 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   36 & 56 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   44 & 48 & (\\text{even}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n9. As each factor pair $(n, k)$ yields valid $(c, b)$ pairs, we have:\n\n\\[\n12 \\, (\\text{distinct right-angled triangles})\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Selecting 3 people from 3 boys and \\( n \\) girls to participate in a competition, given that the probability of having at least one girl among the 3 people is \\( \\frac{34}{35} \\). Find \\( n \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle this probability problem. So, we need to find the number of girls, n, given that the probability of having at least one girl among 3 selected people from 3 boys and n girls is 34/35. Hmm, let me think step by step.\n\nFirst, probability problems often involve combinations. The total number of ways to choose 3 people from (3 boys + n girls) is the combination of (3 + n) taken 3 at a time. The event we're interested in is \"at least one girl\" in the selection. But sometimes, it's easier to calculate the probability of the complementary event and then subtract it from 1. The complementary event here would be selecting no girls, which means all three selected are boys.\n\nRight, so the probability of selecting all boys is the number of ways to choose 3 boys divided by the total number of ways to choose 3 people. Since there are 3 boys, the number of ways to choose all 3 boys is C(3, 3). The total number of ways is C(3 + n, 3). Then, the probability of at least one girl is 1 minus the probability of all boys. According to the problem, this equals 34/35. So, let's set up the equation:\n\n1 - [C(3,3) / C(3 + n, 3)] = 34/35\n\nLet me compute C(3,3). That's 1, since there's only one way to choose all 3 boys. So the equation simplifies to:\n\n1 - [1 / C(3 + n, 3)] = 34/35\n\nSubtracting 1 from both sides gives:\n\n- [1 / C(3 + n, 3)] = 34/35 - 1\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n- [1 / C(3 + n, 3)] = -1/35\n\nMultiplying both sides by -1:\n\n1 / C(3 + n, 3) = 1/35\n\nTherefore, C(3 + n, 3) must equal 35. So, we need to solve for n in the equation:\n\nC(3 + n, 3) = 35\n\nRecall that the combination formula C(k, 3) is k(k - 1)(k - 2)/6. So, substituting k = 3 + n:\n\n( (3 + n)(2 + n)(1 + n) ) / 6 = 35\n\nMultiply both sides by 6:\n\n(3 + n)(2 + n)(1 + n) = 210\n\nHmm, now we need to solve this cubic equation for n. Since n is the number of girls, it has to be a positive integer. Let's try expanding the left side step by step.\n\nFirst, let me compute (1 + n)(2 + n)(3 + n). Let's denote m = n + 1, then it's m(m + 1)(m + 2). Alternatively, we can compute it as:\n\nFirst 