 second? Wait, no. Wait, the first polynomial is 1 + z + 3z\u00b2 + z\u00b3 + z\u2074. The second polynomial is 1 + 1/z + 3/z\u00b2 + 1/z\u00b3 + 1/z\u2074.\n\nSo when we multiply 1 (from first) with 1/z\u2074 (from second), we get 1/z\u2074.\n\nSimilarly, z\u2074 (from first) multiplied by 1 (from second) gives z\u2074.\n\nSo in the expansion, there are two more terms: z\u2074 and 1/z\u2074.\n\nBut in my previous grouping, I missed these. Let me correct that.\n\nSo after collecting all terms:\n\nConstant terms: 13\n\nz terms: 8z\n\n1/z terms: 8/z\n\nz\u00b2 terms: 7z\u00b2\n\n1/z\u00b2 terms: 7/z\u00b2\n\nz\u00b3 terms: 2z\u00b3\n\n1/z\u00b3 terms: 2/z\u00b3\n\nz\u2074 terms: z\u2074\n\n1/z\u2074 terms: 1/z\u2074\n\nTherefore, |P(z)|\u00b2 = 13 + 8z + 8/z + 7z\u00b2 + 7/z\u00b2 + 2z\u00b3 + 2/z\u00b3 + z\u2074 + 1/z\u2074.\n\nBut since |z|=1, we can write z = e^(i\u03b8), so z^k + 1/z^k = 2cos(k\u03b8). Therefore, we can express the entire expression in terms of cosines.\n\nLet me rewrite each term:\n\n8z + 8/z = 8(z + 1/z) = 16cos\u03b8\n\n7z\u00b2 + 7/z\u00b2 = 14cos2\u03b8\n\n2z\u00b3 + 2/z\u00b3 = 4cos3\u03b8\n\nz\u2074 + 1/z\u2074 = 2cos4\u03b8\n\nSo substituting back:\n\n|P(z)|\u00b2 = 13 + 16cos\u03b8 + 14cos2\u03b8 + 4cos3\u03b8 + 2cos4\u03b8\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to minimizing the function f(\u03b8) = 13 + 16cos\u03b8 + 14cos2\u03b8 + 4cos3\u03b8 + 2cos4\u03b8 over \u03b8 \u2208 [0, 2\u03c0).\n\nTo find the minimum value, we can treat this as a real function of \u03b8 and find its critical points by taking the derivative and setting it to zero.\n\nLet me compute the derivative f'(\u03b8):\n\nf'(\u03b8) = -16sin\u03b8 - 28sin2\u03b8 -12sin3\u03b8 -8sin4\u03b8\n\nSet f'(\u03b8) = 0:\n\n-16sin\u03b8 -28sin2\u03b8 -12sin3\u03b8 -8sin4\u03b8 = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n16sin\u03b8 + 28sin2\u03b8 + 12sin3\u03b8 + 8sin4\u03b8 = 0\n\nThis equation needs to be solved for \u03b8. Hmm, this looks complicated. Maybe there's a way to simplify using