he critical points occur when either sin\u03b8 =0 or the expression in the brackets is zero.\n\nCase 1: sin\u03b8 =0. Then \u03b8=0 or \u03b8=\u03c0.\n\nCase 2: 4 +24cos\u03b8 +48cos\u00b2\u03b8 +64cos\u00b3\u03b8 =0\n\nLet me factor out 4:\n\n4[1 +6cos\u03b8 +12cos\u00b2\u03b8 +16cos\u00b3\u03b8] =0\n\nSo 1 +6cos\u03b8 +12cos\u00b2\u03b8 +16cos\u00b3\u03b8 =0\n\nLet me denote x = cos\u03b8. Then the equation becomes:\n\n16x\u00b3 +12x\u00b2 +6x +1 =0\n\nWe need to solve 16x\u00b3 +12x\u00b2 +6x +1 =0\n\nThis is a cubic equation. Let me check if it has rational roots using Rational Root Theorem. Possible roots are \u00b11, \u00b11/2, \u00b11/4, \u00b11/8, \u00b11/16.\n\nTesting x=-1:\n\n16(-1)^3 +12(-1)^2 +6(-1) +1 = -16 +12 -6 +1 = -9 \u22600\n\nx=-1/2:\n\n16(-1/2)^3 +12(-1/2)^2 +6(-1/2) +1 =16*(-1/8) +12*(1/4) +6*(-1/2)+1= -2 +3 -3 +1= -1 \u22600\n\nx=-1/4:\n\n16*(-1/4)^3 +12*(-1/4)^2 +6*(-1/4) +1=16*(-1/64)+12*(1/16)+6*(-1/4)+1= -1/4 + 3/4 - 3/2 +1= (-1/4 +3/4)=1/2; (1/2 -3/2)= -1; (-1 +1)=0. So x=-1/4 is a root!\n\nTherefore, x=-1/4 is a root. Then we can factor the cubic:\n\n16x\u00b3 +12x\u00b2 +6x +1 = (x +1/4)(ax\u00b2 +bx +c)\n\nMultiplying out:\n\n(x +1/4)(ax\u00b2 +bx +c) = ax\u00b3 +bx\u00b2 +cx + (a/4)x\u00b2 + (b/4)x +c/4\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nax\u00b3 + (b + a/4)x\u00b2 + (c + b/4)x + c/4\n\nSet equal to 16x\u00b3 +12x\u00b2 +6x +1, so coefficients:\n\na =16\n\nb + a/4 =12 => b +4=12 => b=8\n\nc + b/4 =6 => c +2=6 => c=4\n\nc/4 =1 => c=4, which matches.\n\nTherefore, 16x\u00b3 +12x\u00b2 +6x +1 = (x +1/4)(16x\u00b2 +8x +4)\n\nSo now, set 16x\u00b2 +8x +4=0. Let's solve this quadratic:\n\nx = [-8 \u00b1 sqrt(64 -256)] /32 = [-8 \u00b1 sqrt(-192)]/32 = [-8 \u00b1 8i\u221a3]/32 = [-1 \u00b1i\u221a3]/4\n\nBut x is supposed to be real (since x=cos\u03b8), so the quadratic has no real roots. Hence, the only real solution is x=-1/4.\n\nThus, Case 2 gives cos\u03b8=-1/4. Therefore, \u03b8= arccos(-1/4). The solutions are \u03b8= \u00b1 arccos(-1/4) +2\u03