1\\) indeed passes through the Brocard points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The circumcircle of triangle } A_1B_1C_1 \\text{ passes through both Brocard points } P \\text{ and } Q.}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo tackle the problem, we need to show that the circumcircle of triangle \\(A_1B_1C_1\\) passes through the Brocard points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) of triangle \\(ABC\\). We proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Introduction to Brocard Points**:\n   - Brocard points are special points in a triangle. Let \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) be the first and second Brocard points of triangle \\(ABC\\). These points have the property that \\(\\angle BPC = \\angle CPA = \\angle APB = \\omega\\) and \\(\\angle BQC = \\angle CQA = \\angle AQB = \\omega\\), where \\(\\omega\\) is the Brocard angle.\n\n2. **Intersection Points**:\n   - Define the points of intersection:\n     \\[\n     A_1 = CP \\cap BQ, \\quad B_1 = AP \\cap CQ, \\quad C_1 = BP \\cap AQ.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Isosceles Triangles**:\n   - Examine the triangles formed by these intersections. We start with triangle \\(AC_1B\\). Given the properties of Brocard points, \\(AB\\) is the base, and \\(\\angle BAC_1 = \\varphi\\) (Brocard angle).\n     - Hence, triangle \\(AC_1B\\) is isosceles with \\(\\angle A_{1} = \\angle BAC = \\varphi\\).\n   - Similarly, consider triangles \\(AB_1C\\) and \\(BC_1A\\), they are also isosceles with \\(\\angle ABC_1 = \\angle BCA_1 = \\varphi\\).\n\n4. **Angles in the Cyclic Quadrilateral**:\n   - Now, since \\(\\angle ACP = \\omega\\) and \\(\\angle BQC = \\omega\\), we have \\(\\angle PCQ = 2\\omega\\).\n   - Similar arguments for other pairs give us \\( \\angle PA_1Q = \\angle PB_1Q = \\angle PC_1Q = 2\\varphi \\).\n\n5. **Cyclic Quadrilaterals**:\n   - Notice that triangles \\(PA_1Q\\), \\(PB_1Q\\), and \\(PC_1Q\\) form cyclic quadrilaterals because their opposite angles add up to \\(180^\\circ\\):\n     - \\(\\angle PA_1Q + \\angle PQC_1 = 2\\varphi + 2\\varphi = 180^\\circ\\).\n\n6