en as x = 1 - (1/2)s, y = 0 + (1/2)s, where s ranges from 0 to 1.\n\nLine KN is from K(0,0) to N(0.5,1). The parametric equations for KN can be written as x = 0.5t, y = t, where t ranges from 0 to 1.\n\nFind the intersection point Q of IH and KN.\n\nSet coordinates equal:\n\n1 - (1/2)s = 0.5t\n\n(1/2)s = t\n\nFrom the second equation, t = (1/2)s. Substitute into first equation:\n\n1 - (1/2)s = 0.5*(1/2)s => 1 - (1/2)s = (1/4)s\n\nMultiply both sides by 4:\n\n4 - 2s = s => 4 = 3s => s = 4/3\n\nThen t = (1/2)*(4/3) = 2/3\n\nTherefore, coordinates of Q are:\n\nx = 1 - (1/2)*(4/3) = 1 - 2/3 = 1/3\n\ny = (1/2)*(4/3) = 2/3\n\nSo Q is (1/3, 2/3).\n\nNow, O is at (2/3, 2/3). So segment OQ is from (2/3, 2/3) to (1/3, 2/3). That's a horizontal line from (2/3,2/3) to (1/3,2/3). Wait, so OQ is horizontal. KI is from K(0,0) to I(1,0), which is also horizontal. Therefore, OQ is parallel to KI. So in this specific case, the conclusion holds.\n\nTherefore, in this coordinate system, it's true. But we need to prove it generally. However, since in the specific case it holds, maybe the general case can be approached similarly.\n\nAlternatively, since we've proven it for a specific case, perhaps the general case can be inferred through similarity or proportionality. But to be rigorous, we need a general proof.\n\nAlternatively, using coordinate geometry in the general case. Let's try again with general coordinates but maybe smarter choices.\n\nLet me place point K at (0,0), point I at (1,0), and point A at (0,c) where c > 1 (since KA = sqrt(0\u00b2 + c\u00b2) = c, and KI = 1, so c > 1 to have KA > KI). Then IA is from (1,0) to (0,c). Midpoint N is (0.5, c/2). The angle bisector of angle K(0,0) meets IA at O. By Angle Bisector Theorem, IO/OA = KI/KA = 1/c. Therefore, O divides IA in ratio IO:OA = 1:c. Therefore, coordinates of O are ( (c*1 + 1*0)/(1 + c), (c*0 + 1*c)/(1 + c) ) = (c/(1 + c), c/(1 + c)).\n\nH is the foot of the perpendicular from I(1,0) to KO. Line KO is from (0,0) to O(c