ld have the same x-coordinate as A, but y=0. Therefore, N is (x, 0). But wait, in that case, the length AN is just the vertical distance from A to N, which is y.\n\nBut then, the problem states that on BC, there is a point M between B and N such that AN = MN and angle BAM = angle NAC. So MN is the length from M to N on BC. Since BC is the x-axis from (0,0) to (12,0), and N is at (x, 0), then M is between B(0,0) and N(x,0). So coordinates of M would be somewhere between 0 and x on the x-axis.\n\nGiven that AN = MN, and AN is the length from A(x,y) to N(x,0), which is y. Then MN is the distance from M to N on BC, which is along the x-axis, so it's |x - m|, where M is at (m, 0). So MN = |x - m|. So given that AN = MN, we have y = |x - m|. But since M is between B and N, m is between 0 and x, so x - m is positive, hence y = x - m, so m = x - y.\n\nAdditionally, the angles angle BAM and angle NAC are equal. Let's denote angle BAM = angle NAC. Let me try to express these angles in terms of coordinates.\n\nPoint A is at (x,y), B is at (0,0), M is at (m, 0) = (x - y, 0). Point C is at (12,0), N is at (x,0). So angle BAM is the angle at point A between points B and M. Similarly, angle NAC is the angle at point A between points N and C.\n\nTo find these angles, perhaps we can use vectors or slopes.\n\nFirst, let's compute vectors AB and AM for angle BAM. Vector AB is from A(x,y) to B(0,0), which is (-x, -y). Vector AM is from A(x,y) to M(x - y, 0), which is (-y, -y). Similarly, angle NAC is the angle between vectors AN and AC. Vector AN is from A(x,y) to N(x,0), which is (0, -y). Vector AC is from A(x,y) to C(12,0), which is (12 - x, -y).\n\nThe angle between two vectors can be found using the dot product formula:\n\ncos(theta) = (u \u00b7 v) / (|u| |v|)\n\nSo for angle BAM between vectors AB and AM:\n\ncos(angle BAM) = [AB \u00b7 AM] / (|AB| |AM|)\n\nSimilarly, for angle NAC between vectors AN and AC:\n\ncos(angle NAC) = [AN \u00b7 AC] / (|AN| |AC|)\n\nGiven that angle BAM =