 find when she bakes on Thursday again, we seek the least common multiple (LCM) of 5 and 7, which determines when the cycles align.\n\n**Calculation**:\n- LCM of 5 and 7 is \\( 5 \\times 7 = 35 \\), since 5 and 7 are coprime.\n\n**Verification**:\n- Starting from any day, after 35 days, the combination of 5-day baking cycles and 7-day weeks repeats, ensuring she bakes on the same day of the week again.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nRegardless of the starting day, Amrita will next bake on a Thursday in **35 days**.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{35}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify Baking Cycle and Week Duration:**\n   - Amrita bakes a cake every 5 days.\n   - Thursdays occur every 7 days.\n\n2. **Understand the Problem:**\n   - We need to find the next day when both these cycles coincide, i.e., the day Amrita will bake on a Thursday.\n\n3. **Determine the Mathematical Condition:**\n   - We need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the two cycles (5 and 7).\n   - The LCM is the smallest number that is a multiple of both 5 and 7.\n\n4. **Calculate the LCM:**\n   - To find the LCM of 5 and 7, list the multiples of each number:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &\\text{Multiples of 5:} \\quad 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, \\ldots \\\\\n     &\\text{Multiples of 7:} \\quad 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, \\ldots\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n   - The smallest common multiple in the lists above is 35.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - The least common multiple (LCM) of 5 and 7 is 35.\n   - Thus, Amrita will next bake on a Thursday in 35 days.\n\n\\[ \\boxed{E} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Shaded and unshaded squares are arranged in rows so that:\n\n- the first row consists of one unshaded square,\n- each row begins with an unshaded square,\n- the squares in each row alternate between unshaded and shaded, and\n- each row after the first has two more squares than the previous row.\n\nThe first 4 rows are