2} \\right) = -4 (x+2)^{-3} = \\frac{-4}{(x + 2)^3}\n   $$\n   Since \\(h''(x) < 0\\) for all \\(x > 0\\), \\( h(x) \\) is concave.\n\n6. Since \\(h(x)\\) is a concave function, by Jensen's Inequality, we have:\n   $$\n   \\sum x h(2 y - z) \\leq h \\left( \\sum x (2 y - z) \\right)\n   $$\n\n7. Use the constraint \\( x + y + z = 1 \\) to apply the given transformations:\n   $$\n   h \\left( \\sum x (2 y - z) \\right) = h \\left( \\sum xy \\right)\n   $$\n\n8. Apply the inequality known from the arithmetic mean-geometric mean (AM-GM) inequality:\n   $$\n   xy + yz + zx \\leq \\frac{(x + y + z)^2}{3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   $$\n\n9. Since \\(h(x)\\) is an increasing function:\n   $$\n   h\\left( \\sum xy \\right) \\leq h \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{2 + \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1/3}{7/3} = \\frac{1}{7}\n   $$\n\n10. Therefore:\n    $$\n    f(x, y, z) \\leq \\frac{1}{7}\n    $$\n    \n11. Equality holds when \\(x = y = z = \\frac{1}{3}\\):\n    $$\n    f\\left( \\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{1}{3} \\right) = \\frac{1}{7}\n    $$\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{7}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(a, b, c\\) be positive real numbers. Prove that \\(a^{2a} b^{2b} c^{2c} \\geqslant a^{b+c} b^{c+a} c^{a+b}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that \\(a^{2a} b^{2b} c^{2c} \\geqslant a^{b+c} b^{c+a} c^{a+b}\\) where \\(a, b, c\\) are positive real numbers. Hmm, let's start by trying to simplify the inequality. Maybe take logarithms on both sides to turn the exponents into coefficients, which might make it easier to handle.\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\(\\ln(a^{2a} b^{2b} c^{2c}) \\geqslant \\ln(a^{b+c} b^{c+a} c^{a+b})\\)\n\nUsing logarithm properties, this becomes:\n\n\\(2a \\ln a + 2b \\ln b + 2c \\ln c \\geq (b + c)\\ln a + (c + a)\\ln b + (a + b)\\ln c\\)\n\nLet me rearrange the terms on both sides. Let's subtract the right-hand side from both sides to bring everything to one side:\n\n\\(2a \\ln a + 2b \\ln b + 2c \\ln c - (b + c)\\ln a - (c + a)\\ln b - (a + b)\\ln c \\geq 0\\)\n\nNow, let's group like terms. For the coefficients of \\(\\ln a\\), \\(\\ln b\\), and \\(\\ln c\\):\n\nFor \\(\\ln a\\):\n\\(2a \\ln a - (b + c)\\ln a = (2a - b - c)\\ln a\\)\n\nSimilarly for \\(\\ln b\\):\n\\(2b \\ln b - (c + a)\\ln b = (2b - c - a)\\ln b\\)\n\nAnd for \\(\\ln c\\):\n\\(2c \\ln c - (a + b)\\ln c = (2c - a - b)\\ln c\\)\n\nSo the inequality becomes:\n\n\\((2a - b - c)\\ln a + (2b - c - a)\\ln b + (2c - a - b)\\ln c \\geq 0\\)\n\nHmm, this looks a bit complicated. Maybe there's a symmetry here or a way to apply known inequalities like AM-GM or Weighted AM-GM. Alternatively, maybe rearrangement inequality?\n\nWait, another approach: perhaps divide both sides of the original inequality by \\(a^{b+c} b^{c+a} c^{a+b}\\) to get:\n\n\\(a^{2a - b - c} b^{2b - c - a} c^{2c - a - b} \\geq 1\\)\n\nWhich can be written as:\n\n\\(\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^{a - b} \\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right)^{b - c} \\left(\\frac{c}{a}\\right)^{c - a} \\geq 1\\)\n\nWait, let me check that. Let me rewrite the exponents:\n\nFor \\(a\\), the exponent is \\(2a - b - c = (a - b) + (a - c)\\)\n\nSimilarly for \\(b\\): \\(2b - c - a = (b - c) + (b - a)\\)\n\nAnd for \\(c\\): \\(2c - a - b = (c - a) + (c - b)\\)\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\(a^{(a - b) + (a - c)} b^{(b - c) + (b - a)} c^{(c - a) + (c - b)}\\)\n\nWhich can be grouped as:\n\n\\(\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^{a - b} \\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right)^{b - c} \\left(\\frac{c}{a}\\right)^{c - a}\\)\n\nYes, that's correct. So the inequality is equivalent to:\n\n\\(\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^{a - b} \\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right)^{b - c} \\left(\\frac{c}{a}\\right)^{c - a} \\geq 1\\)\n\nHmm, this seems more manageable. Let's take the natural logarithm of both sides again to analyze:\n\n\\((a - b)\\ln\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) + (b - c)\\ln\\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right) + (