= s b, where s \u2208 [-1,1].\n\nThe intersection of the circle |x|\u00b2 = |h|\u00b2 with BC is when |s b|\u00b2 = |h|\u00b2 \u21d2 s\u00b2 |b|\u00b2 = |h|\u00b2 \u21d2 s = \u00b1|h| / |b|.\n\nTherefore, the points A1 and A2 are at s = \u00b1|h| / |b|, so x = \u00b1 (|h| / |b|) b.\n\nBut since BC is from -b to b, the points A1 and A2 are at (|h| / |b|) b and -(|h| / |b|) b.\n\nSimilarly, for other sides.\n\nNow, to find if these points lie on a common circle, we can check the equation of a circle in vectors.\n\nA general circle equation in vector terms can be written as |x - c|\u00b2 = r\u00b2, where c is the center vector and r is the radius.\n\nSuppose all six points lie on such a circle. Then for each point x, |x - c|\u00b2 = r\u00b2.\n\nTake points A1 and A2: | \u00b1 (|h| / |b|) b - c |\u00b2 = r\u00b2.\n\nSimilarly for points on other sides. This approach might be too abstract. Maybe instead, use the properties of midpoints and orthocenter.\n\nAnother idea: Since all three circles pass through H, their radical axes are the perpendicular bisectors of the segments joining H to the midpoints. But not sure.\n\nAlternatively, note that the six points lie on the circle if and only if their reflections over the midpoints also lie on the circle. Since each pair (A1, A2) are reflections over M_a, etc., the circle must be invariant under reflection over M_a, M_b, M_c. Therefore, the center of the circle must be the common intersection of the perpendicular bisectors, which is the circumcenter. Hence, the circle is centered at the circumcenter.\n\nTherefore, if we can show that the distance from the circumcenter O to each of the six points is the same, then they lie on a circle centered at O.\n\nIn our specific example, this was approximately true. Let's verify in our specific case:\n\nCircumcenter O was at (0,0.75). Distance to A1 (0.5,0): sqrt(0.5\u00b2 +0.75\u00b2)=sqrt(0.8125)=0.901. Distance to B1 (0.816,0.368): sqrt(0.816\u00b2 + (0.75 -0.368)\u00b2)=sqrt(0.666 +0.