in \\beta\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha - \\gamma) + \\cos(\\beta - \\gamma) = -\\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nConsidering the angles \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\) as vectors on the unit circle, their sum is zero, implying they form an equilateral triangle. For such vectors, the sum of the squares of the cosines is constant. \n\nUsing the identity for the sum of squares:\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\beta + \\cos^2 \\gamma = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\cos^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\beta + \\cos^2 \\gamma\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to find the value of $\\cos^{2} \\alpha + \\cos^{2} \\beta + \\cos^{2} \\gamma$ given the conditions:\n\\[ \\sin \\alpha + \\sin \\beta + \\sin \\gamma = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma = 0 \\]\n\n1. From the given conditions, we can rewrite:\n   \\[ \\sin \\alpha + \\sin \\beta = -\\sin \\gamma \\]\n   \\[ \\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta = -\\cos \\gamma \\]\n\n2. We square both equations and then add them:\n   \\[\n   (\\sin \\alpha + \\sin \\beta)^2 + (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta)^2 = (-\\sin \\gamma)^2 + (-\\cos \\gamma)^2\n   \\]\n\n3. We simplify the right side:\n   \\[\n   (-\\sin \\gamma)^2 + (-\\cos \\gamma)^2 = \\sin^2 \\gamma + \\cos^2 \\gamma = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. Expanding and adding the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   (\\sin \\alpha + \\sin \\beta)^2 + (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta)^2 = \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta + 2\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta + \\cos^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\beta + 2\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta\n   \\]\n\n5. Use the Pythagorean identity:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\cos^2 \\alpha = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sin^2 \\beta + \\cos^2 \\beta = 1\n   \\]\n\n6. So we have:\n   \\[\n   1 + 1 + 2(\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta + \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta) = 1\n   \\]\n\n7. This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   2 + 2\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) = 1\n   \\]\n\n8. Solving for $\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta)$:\n   \\[\n   2\\cos(\\alpha - \\be