+ C = 180^\\circ \\]\n   \\[ A + C = 120^\\circ \\]\n\n4. We need to simplify the given trigonometric expressions. We start with:\n   \\[ \\tan(60^\\circ - C) \\]\n\n   Since \\( A + C = 120^\\circ \\), we can write:\n   \\[ 60^\\circ = \\frac{A + C}{2} \\]\n\n   Therefore,\n   \\[ \\tan(60^\\circ - C) = \\tan\\left(\\frac{A + C}{2} - C\\right) = \\tan\\frac{A - C}{2} \\]\n\n5. Simplify the terms involving sine:\n   \\[ \\sin A - \\sin C = 2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{A + C}{2}\\right) \\]\n\n   Since \\( A + C = 120^\\circ \\) and hence:\n   \\[ \\frac{A + C}{2} = 60^\\circ \\]\n   \\[ \\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\n   Thus,\n   \\[ \\sin A - \\sin C = 2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\sin\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) \\]\n\n6. Similarly,\n   \\[ \\sin A + \\sin C = 2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{A + C}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) \\]\n   \n   Given:\n   \\[ \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\]\n\n   Thus,\n   \\[ \\sin A + \\sin C = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) = \\sqrt{3} \\cos\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) \\]\n\n7. Using the simplified forms, we rewrite the equations of the lines:\n\n   For $l_{1}$:\n   \\[ x + \\frac{\\sin A + \\sin C}{\\sqrt{3}} y + 1 = 0 \\]\n   \\[ x + \\cos\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) y + 1 = 0 \\]\n\n   For $l_{2}$:\n   \\[ x \\cdot \\tan\\left(60^\\circ - C\\right) + y (\\sin A - \\sin C) - \\tan\\frac{C - A}{2} = 0 \\]\n   \\[ x \\cdot \\tan\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) + y \\sin\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) - \\tan\\left(\\frac{C - A}{2}\\right) = 0 \\]\n\n8. Since $\\tan\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\frac{C - A}{2}\\right)$, we have:\n   \\[ x + y \\cos\\left(\\frac{A - C}{2}\\right) + 1 = 0 \\]\n\n   Both lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ can be simplified to the same equation. Hence, these lines are coincident (overlapping).\n\nConclusion:\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "