s sheet includes Z and W (if Z's sheet includes W). Therefore, X's sheet would be Y, Z, W. So it's like the transitive closure. Because you take all the people you defeated, and all the people they defeated, etc. So effectively, the sheet is all the people that can be reached by following the defeats starting from X. Therefore, the sheet of X is the set of all players that X can reach via a directed path in the tournament graph.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to showing that in a tournament graph, there exists a node X such that every other node is reachable from X via a directed path. Such a node is called a \"king\" in tournament terminology, but I think more accurately, it's a node with out-degree n-1 in the transitive closure. However, in tournaments, it's known that there is always at least one king (a node that can reach every other node in at most two steps), which would imply that such a node's sheet includes all other names. Therefore, the result follows from the known theorem in tournament theory.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to prove it from scratch. Let's consider the tournament graph where each node represents a player, and an edge from A to B means A defeated B. Now, the sheet of a player A is the set of all nodes reachable from A. We need to show that there exists a player A such that every other node is reachable from A.\n\nTo prove this, consider the concept of a strongly connected component (SCC) in the tournament. A tournament is either strongly connected or can be divided into SCCs where there is a directed edge from one SCC to another. However, in a tournament, the SCCs form a linear order where each SCC has edges to all subsequent SCCs. But in a tournament, if it's not strongly connected, then it can be partitioned into two subsets S and T such that all edges go from S to T. Wait, actually, in general tournaments, the structure can be more complex, but the key point is that tournaments are strongly connected or have a dominating node.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: Consider the player who has the maximum number of victories. Let's say player A has the most wins. Suppose A has beaten k players. If k = n-1, then A has beaten everyone, so their sheet includes everyone. If not, then there exists some player B that A lost to. Now, B must have beaten A, and since A has the maximum number of victories, B can't have more victories than A. Therefore, B has beaten some players, but perhaps fewer than A. However, since B has beaten A, and A has beaten more players than B, then perhaps the players that B has beaten are a subset of those that A has beaten. Wait, not necessarily. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, consider that in a tournament, for any two players A and B, either A beats B or vice versa. So if we take a player A who is a king, then A can reach every other player in at most two steps. Therefore, A's sheet would include all players, because if A beats someone directly, they're on the sheet, and if A doesn't beat them, then A beats someone who beats them, so they would be on the sheet of that someone, hence on A's sheet. Therefore, the king's sheet includes everyone. Therefore, since every tournament has a king, there must be someone with all names on their sheet.\n\nAlternatively, let's try a proof by induction. Assume that for all tournaments with k < n players, there exists a player whose sheet includes all others. Now consider a tournament with n players. Take any player, say A. If A has beaten everyone, then A's sheet includes everyone. If not, then there exists some player B that A lost to. Now, consider the subtournament consisting of all players except A. By induction hypothesis, there is a player C in this subtournament whose sheet (within the subtournament) includes all other players in the subtournament. Now, if C beat A, then C's sheet in the entire tournament would include A (directly) and all others from the subtournament (by induction), so C's sheet includes everyone. If C did not beat A, then A beat C. But since A lost to B, and B is in the subtournament, then C's sheet in the subtournament includes B. Therefore, C's sheet in the entire tournament would include B and everyone else in the subtournament. Since A beat C, then A's sheet includes C and everyone on C's sheet. Since C's sheet includes B and everyone else in the subtournament, then A's sheet includes C, B, and the rest. But A was beaten by B, so B is not on A's sheet directly. Wait, this is confusing. Maybe induction isn't the best approach here.\n\nWait, let's step back. If we model the tournament as a directed graph, then the sheets correspond to the closure of each node under the relation \"defeated directly or by someone they defeated\". Therefore, the sheet of a player is the set of all players that can be reached by following the directed edges from that player. So we need to show that there exists a player from whom all other players are reachable. This is equivalent to saying that the tournament has at least one node with out-degree n-1 in the transitive closure. \n\nIn graph theory, a tournament is a strongly connected if for every pair of vertices u and v, there's a directed path from u to v. However, not all tournaments are strongly connected. But even if a tournament isn't strongly connected, it can be decomposed into strongly connected components, and there is a component that can reach all others. Wait, but how does that help?\n\nAlternatively, consider that in any tournament, there is always a Hamiltonian path. That is, a sequence of players where each player beats the next one in the sequence. The last player in this Hamiltonian path can reach every other player through the path. Wait, but how? If there's a Hamiltonian path A1 -> A2 -> ... -> An, then An is the last player. But An can only reach the players that come after him in the path, but there are none. Wait, no. In the Hamiltonian path, each Ai beats Ai+1. So An is beaten by An-1, but An might beat some earlier players. Wait, no, in a Hamiltonian path, it's a linear order where each beats the next. So if you have a Hamiltonian path, the first player can reach everyone else through the path. For example, A1 beats A2, who beats A3, etc. So A1 can reach A2, A3, ..., An via the path. However, A1 might not have beaten A3 directly, but since A2 beats A3, and A1 beats A2, then A1's sheet includes A2 and A3 (through A2's sheet). Similarly, A1 can reach everyone down the path. Therefore, the first player in the Hamiltonian path would have a sheet containing all names. Hence, if we can show that the first player in a Hamiltonian path has this property, then we're done. Since every tournament has a Hamiltonian path, this would suffice.\n\nYes, this seems like a solid approach. Every tournament has a Hamiltonian path. Let's take such a path: P = v1, v2, ..., vn, where each vi beats vi+1. Now, consider the first player v1. We need to show that v1's sheet contains all other players. For any player vj (j > 1), either v1 beats vj directly, or there is some k < j such that v1 beats vk and vk beats vj. Since P is a Hamiltonian path, v1 beats v2, v2 beats v3, and so on. Therefore, for any vj, there is a path from v1 to vj through the path: v1 -> v2 -> ... -> vj. Hence, v1 can reach vj through a chain of victories. Therefore, vj will be on v1's sheet because v1 defeated v2, who defeated v3, ..., up to vj. Hence, all names are included in v1's sheet. Therefore, v1 is the player with all names on their sheet.\n\nWait, but is this necessarily true? Let's see. Suppose v1's sheet includes v2 (direct) and then v2's sheet includes v3 (direct), so v1's sheet gets v3. Then v3's sheet includes v4, so v1's sheet also includes v4, etc. So recursively, v1's sheet would include all the way up to vn. Therefore, yes, v1's sheet would include all players. Hence, the existence of a Hamiltonian path implies the existence of such a player. Therefore, since every tournament has a Hamiltonian path, we can conclude that such a player exists.\n\nTherefore, the key steps are:\n\n1. Model the tournament as a directed graph where an edge from A to B means A defeated B.\n\n2. Recall that every tournament has a Hamiltonian path.\n\n3. The first vertex in this Hamiltonian path can reach every other vertex through the path.\n\n4. Therefore, the sheet of this first vertex will include all other players' names, either directly or through the sheets of defeated opponents.\n\nHence, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the problem, we model the tournament as a directed graph (tournament graph) where each node represents a participant, and a directed edge from node \\( A \\) to \\( B \\) indicates \\( A \\) defeated \\( B \\). The key is to show there exists a participant whose sheet contains all other names. This is equivalent to proving there exists a node from which all other nodes are reachable via directed paths.\n\n### Key Steps:\n1. **Hamiltonian Path in Tournaments**: Every tournament graph contains a Hamiltonian path\u2014a sequence of nodes where each node beats the next node in the sequence. Let this path be \\( v_1 \\rightarrow v_2 \\rightarrow \\cdots \\rightarrow v_n \\).\n\n2. **Reachability from the First Node**: Consider the first node \\( v_1 \\) in the Hamiltonian path. By the structure of the path:\n   - \\( v_1 \\) directly defeats \\( v_2 \\).\n   - \\( v_2 \\) directly defeats \\( v_3 \\), so \\( v_1 \\)'s sheet includes \\( v_3 \\) via \\( v_2 \\)'s sheet.\n   - Inductively, \\( v_1 \\) can reach \\( v_j \\) (\\( j \\geq 2 \\)) through the chain \\( v_1 \\rightarrow v_2 \\rightarrow \\cdots \\rightarrow v_j \\).\n\n3. **Transitive Closure**: Since \\( v_1 \\)'s sheet includes all names from the sheets of those it directly defeated (and recursively their sheets), \\( v_1 \\) accumulates all names along the Hamiltonian path. Thus, \\( v_1 \\)'s sheet contains every other participant's name.\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy leveraging the existence of a Hamiltonian path in tournaments, we conclude that the first participant in such a path must have all other names on their sheet. Therefore, **there always exists a participant with all other names on their sheet**.\n\n**Answer:**  \nIn any such tournament, there must exist a participant whose sheet includes all others. This follows because every tournament has a Hamiltonian path, and the first participant in this path indirectly defeats everyone through subsequent victories, ensuring all names appear on their sheet. \n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Such a participant always exists.}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Understanding the Problem:** After a Gobbit tournament, each player has played at least one game with every other participant. Each player writes on a sheet the names of opponents they have defeated and the names of the people on their defeated opponents' sheets, ensuring that no one writes their own name.\n\n2. **Choose Person \\( A \\):** Consider a person \\( A \\) who has the maximum number of names on their sheet.\n\n    - Let \\( S(A) \\) represent the set of names on \\( A \\)'s sheet.\n    - By assumption, \\( |S(A)| \\) (the number of names on \\( A \\)'s sheet) is maximized.\n\n3. **Assume \\( A \\) Does Not Have All Names:** Suppose \\( A \\)\u2019s sheet does not contain the name of some player \\( C \\).\n\n4. **Analyze the Game Between \\( A \\) and \\( C \\):**\n    - Since \\( C \\) is not on \\( A \\)\u2019s sheet, \\( A \\) did not defeat \\( C \\).\n    - Therefore, \\( C \\) must have defeated \\( A \\) in the game they played.\n\n5. **Names on \\( C \\)'s Sheet:**\n    - \\( C \\)\u2019s sheet must contain \\( A \\) since \\( C \\) defeated \\( A \\).\n    - In addition, \\( C \\)\u2019s sheet contains all the names on \\( A \\)\u2019s sheet because defeating \\( A \\) means \\( C \\) adopts all the names from \\( A \\)'s sheet.\n\n6. **Comparison of Name Sets:**\n    - The set of names on \\( C \\)\u2019s sheet, \\( S(C) \\), includes all names in \\( S(A) \\) plus at least the name \\( A \\). Formally, \\( S(C) \\supseteq S(A) \\cup \\{A\\} \\).\n\n7. **Contradiction:**\n    - Given \\( A \\) was supposed to have the maximum number of names, but \\( C \\) ends up with a strictly larger set of names, this contradicts the earlier assumption.\n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n    - Therefore, the initial assumption that \\( A \\) does not have all the names must be false.\n    - Hence, \\( A \\) must have all the other players' names on their sheet.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Someone has all the other names on their sheet.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Determine the values of \\(\\lambda\\) for which the function\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x^{2}-6 x+5}{x^{2}+4 x+\\lambda}\n$$\n\ndoes not have any extrema. Examine the behavior of this function and plot it for \\(\\lambda = -12\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. The question is asking for the values of \u03bb for which the function y = (x\u00b2 - 6x + 5)/(x\u00b2 + 4x + \u03bb) does not have any extrema. Then, we need to examine its behavior and plot it when \u03bb = -12. \n\nFirst, to find when a function has no extrema, we need to look at its derivative and determine when the derivative has no real zeros. If the derivative never equals zero, then the function doesn't have any local maxima or minima. So, my plan is to find the derivative of y with respect to x, set it equal to zero, and then find the conditions on \u03bb such that this equation has no real solutions. \n\nLet me start by recalling the quotient rule for derivatives. If we have a function y = f(x)/g(x), then the derivative y' = (f'(x)g(x) - f(x)g'(x))/[g(x)]\u00b2. Applying this to our function:\n\nf(x) = x\u00b2 - 6x + 5, so f'(x) = 2x - 6.\n\ng(x) = x\u00b2 + 4x + \u03bb, so g'(x) = 2x + 4.\n\nTherefore, the derivative y' is:\n\n[(2x - 6)(x\u00b2 + 4x + \u03bb) - (x\u00b2 - 6x + 5)(2x + 4)] / (x\u00b2 + 4x + \u03bb)\u00b2.\n\nNow, let's simplify the numerator:\n\nFirst term: (2x - 6)(x\u00b2 + 4x + \u03bb)\n\nLet's expand this:\n\n2x*(x\u00b2) + 2x*(4x) + 2x*\u03bb - 6*(x\u00b2) - 6*(4x) - 6*\u03bb\n\n= 2x\u00b3 + 8x\u00b2 + 2\u03bbx - 6x\u00b2 - 24x - 6\u03bb\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n2x\u00b3 + (8x\u00b2 - 6x\u00b2) + (2\u03bbx - 24x) - 6\u03bb\n\n= 2x\u00b3 + 2x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 24)x - 6\u03bb\n\nSecond term: -(x\u00b2 - 6x + 5)(2x + 4)\n\nLet's expand this:\n\n- [x\u00b2*(2x) + x\u00b2*4 - 6x*(2x) - 6x*4 + 5*(2x) + 5*4]\n\n= - [2x\u00b3 + 4x\u00b2 - 12x\u00b2 - 24x + 10x + 20]\n\nSimplify inside the brackets:\n\n2x\u00b3 + (4x\u00b2 - 12x\u00b2) + (-24x + 10x) + 20\n\n= 2x\u00b3 - 8x\u00b2 - 14x + 20\n\nSo, multiplying by -1:\n\n-2x\u00b3 + 8x\u00b2 + 14x - 20\n\nNow, combine the two terms:\n\nFirst term: 2x\u00b3 + 2x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 24)x - 6\u03bb\n\nSecond term: -2x\u00b3 + 8x\u00b2 + 14x - 20\n\nAdding them together:\n\n(2x\u00b3 - 2x\u00b3) + (2x\u00b2 + 8x\u00b2) + [(2\u03bb - 24)x + 14x] + (-6\u03bb - 20)\n\n= 0x\u00b3 + 10x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 24 + 14)x - 6\u03bb - 20\n\nSimplify the coefficients:\n\n10x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 10)x - 6\u03bb - 20\n\nSo, the numerator of y' is 10x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 10)x - 6\u03bb - 20.\n\nTherefore, the critical points occur where 10x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 10)x - 6\u03bb - 20 = 0.\n\nFor the function y to have no extrema, this quadratic equation in x must have no real solutions. A quadratic equation ax\u00b2 + bx + c = 0 has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative. The discriminant D is b\u00b2 - 4ac.\n\nSo, let's compute the discriminant of 10x\u00b2 + (2\u03bb - 10)x - 6\u03bb - 20 = 0.\n\nHere, a = 10, b = (2\u03bb - 10), c = -6\u03bb - 20.\n\nThus, D = (2\u03bb - 10)\u00b2 - 4 * 10 * (-6\u03bb - 20)\n\nFirst, compute (2\u03bb - 10)\u00b2:\n\n= 4\u03bb\u00b2 - 40\u03bb + 100\n\nThen compute 4 * 10 * (-6\u03bb -20):\n\n= 40*(-6\u03bb -20) = -240\u03bb -800\n\nSo, D = 4\u03bb\u00b2 -40\u03bb +100 - [ -240\u03